/p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Br</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>MnO</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>MnO</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>MnSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mi>HOCl</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<blockquote><p>The metal halide can be manganese dichloride. It gives white precipitate of manganese hydroxide.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>MnCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>NaOH</mi></mrow></mover><munder><mrow><mi>Mn</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfenced></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mi>NaCl</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></mfenced></munder></math></p><p>The white precipitate manganese hydroxide on reaction with lead dioxide and concentrated sulphuric acid gives purple coloured solution permanganate.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mn</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><munderover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>Conc</mi><mo>.</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>heat</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>PbO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mi>excess</mi></mfenced></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>MnO</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup><mfenced><mrow><mi>purple</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solution</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>The sodium chloride gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> on reaction with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MnO</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>conc</mi><mo>.</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>. The chlorine formed can oxidise iodide to iodine which gives blue colouration with starch paper.</p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"JEE Advanced 2023 (Paper 1)","_marksId":"64b16aa2f23ac7d519f9b634","_advSection":"SC","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect","_originalQ":"<p>In the scheme given below, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">Y</mi></math>, respectively, are</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>Metal halide</mtext><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>. </mo><mi>NaOH</mi></mrow></mover><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mtext> White precipitate</mtext><mo> </mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mo> </mo><mtext>Filtrate</mtext><mo> </mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo mathvariant=\"bold\"> </mo><mo mathvariant=\"bold\"> </mo><mo mathvariant=\"bold\"> </mo><munder><mover><mrow><mover><mo>&#10230;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>PbO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mo>(</mo><mi>excess</mi><mo>) </mo></mrow></mover><mo mathvariant=\"bold\"> </mo><mo mathvariant=\"bold\"> </mo></mrow><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>aq</mi><mo>. </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mover><mro